<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">New Hebrides Condominium</h1><p class="st-meta">1906 CE – 1980 CE · capital Port Vila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A unique joint colony ruled by both Britain and France for 74 years, with two governments, two police forces and two legal systems, which became Vanuatu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French and British settlers, planters and missionaries competed in the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> from the mid-19th century, and neither power would let the other annex the islands. After a joint naval commission was set up in 1887, an Anglo-French convention of 1906 created a condominium, refined by a protocol of 1914.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each power ran its own administration, police, schools, currency and courts for its own nationals, while a Joint Court, originally presided over by a judge appointed by the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, handled land and mixed cases. Ni-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> had no citizenship. Plantations produced copra and cocoa. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a> became a huge American base, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-frum-movement">John Frum movement</a> spread on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanna">Tanna</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuaaku-pati">Vanuaʻaku Pati</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-lini">Walter Lini</a> led the drive for independence, opposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagriamel">Nagriamel</a> movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-stevens">Jimmy Stevens</a>, which attempted secession on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/espiritu-santo">Espiritu Santo</a> in 1980. The New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> on 30 July 1980.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-hebrides-condominium.jpg" alt="New Hebrides Condominium" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vanuatu_on_the_globe_(Polynesia_centered).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1906 CE – 1980 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Vila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walter Lini</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Presbyterianism, Roman Catholicism, Anglicanism, Indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bislama, English, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vanuatu</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Hebrides, Nouvelles-Hébrides</dd></dl></aside></div>

